      "intro": "If your loved one lives in a Connecticut nursing home, assisted living, or residential care facility and has problems with care, billing, or treatment, the Long Term Care Ombudsman provides free advocacy. Ombudsmen investigate complaints confidentially, help resolve disputes between residents and facilities, and ensure your loved one's rights are protected.\n\nThe program serves residents of all licensed long-term care facilities statewide, regardless of income. Because ombudsmen are independent advocates (not employed by facilities), they can address issues like inadequate care, medication errors, billing disputes, or restrictions on visitors without conflict of interest.\n\nFamilies considering facility placement can also get information about quality ratings and resident rights before choosing where their loved one will live.",
